1,2 $ a Annulus typically present (veil membranous, even when annulus not present there are partial veil remnants hanging from the cap margin in young specimens)
1,8 $ b Annulus absent (if a veil is present it is fibrillose)
2,3 $ a Growing on dung of herbivores
2,4 $ b Habitat other than the above
3,0 $ a Stipe below annulus faintly fibrillose to glabrous; spores 8-10(11) x 5-6 um $ Conocybe stercoraria
3,0 $ b Stipe below annulus fibrillose with buff fibrils; spores 7-9 x 4-4.5 um $ Conocybe fimicola
4,0 $ a (2b) Stipe often flexuous, annulus with a thick cottony margin, growing on debris from an avalanche in a cold wet mountain habitat; spores 9-11 x 5-5.5 um $ Conocybe flexipes
4,5 $ b Not as above
5,0 $ a Cap viscid when young, stipe densely fibrillose with pale buff fibrils below annulus, spores 7-9 x 4-4.5 um $ Conocybe pinguis
5,6 $ b Cap not viscid, stipe fibrils pale buff, silvery, or yellow-brown, sparse to woolly-floccose, spores various sizes
6,0 $ a Annulus typically well-formed and stipe not floccose overall $ Conocybe filaris group
6,7 $ b Annulus seldom well-formed; stipe floccose overall
7,0 $ a Stipe 1-3 mm wide $ Conocybe brunnea
7,0 $ b Stipe 3-5 mm wide $ Conocybe fibrillosipes
8,9 $ a (1b) Stipe greenish to bluish near or at the base
8,10 $ b Base of stipe not greenish
9,0 $ a Cheilocystidia fusoid-ventricose $ Conocybe cyanopus
9,0 $ b Cheilocystidia subcapitate $ Conocybe smithii
10,0 $ a (8b) Cap 1-2.5 cm broad and tall, brown to cinnamon-brown or yellow-brown, fading, margin finely striate when moist, gills close, stipe thin, fragile, growing in a variety of habitats but not generally on wood $ Conocybe tenera group
10,11 $ b Cap white overall or with buff center, or if cap brown then growing on wood
11,0 $ a Cap white overall or buff on disc, gills narrow and close, stipe very fragile (fruitbody often lasting only a few hours before toppling over or withering), growing in grass $ Conocybe albipes
11,0 $ b Cap tawny, gills broad, stipe fragile, on rotten conifer logs $ Conocybe aberrans
